A tinySA, as a second opinion
=============================

tinysa.py reads a tinySA over its USB serial line.  It is here for one
reason: os/inferno/radio/cc1120.c and the qemu model it was developed
against were written by the same hand, and a receiver reporting its
own signal strength is a board agreeing with itself.  A tinySA is an
instrument somebody else built, with its own front end, its own
calibration and its own reasons to be wrong.

	tools/tinysa/tinysa.py sweep 868000000 928000000 >tsa.txt
	tools/tinysa/tinysa.py cmd version rbw attenuate

and on the board, over the same band:

	; echo scan >/dev/radio0/ctl

Both are frequency-and-level, so the comparison is awk(1).  The two do
not measure the same thing and are not expected to agree in absolute
terms: a tinySA ULTRA at 100kHz resolution bandwidth and no input
attenuation is a calibrated receiver, while the CC1120's RSSI is its
own AGC's estimate with the manufacturer's reference offset applied
and no calibration of this board at all.  What is worth comparing is
where the energy is and whether it stays there, not how much of it
there is.

The reference instrument needs checking too
-------------------------------------------

The first thing this tool measured was nothing at all, convincingly.

The tinySA stops sweeping while it serves commands on the serial line,
and "data 0" hands back whatever is in the trace buffer.  If nothing
has swept since, that is the previous sweep -- or the sweep of
whatever span the thing was showing before the range was set.  Read
that way it looks exactly like a measurement.  Three consecutive reads
agreed to the last digit: r = 1.000 between every pair, 0 of 450
points differing, a confident plateau at 868.13 to 868.80MHz at -71 to
-77dBm.

None of which was in the band it appeared to be in, because the trace
predated the "sweep 868000000 928000000" that was supposed to have
produced it.

What caught it was the same test the board's own sweeps had to pass: a
repeat.  Thermal noise measured twice does not agree at r = 1.000.
Every trace is now taken with resume, wait, pause around it, which
runs exactly one sweep and stops at the end of it, and two sweeps of
the same quiet band through the tool now read r = 0.287 with 35 of 450
points alike, which is what a repeat is supposed to look like.

There is a lesson in that worth more than the measurement: an
instrument brought in to check another instrument is not automatically
the more trustworthy of the two.

The tinySA's own formatter, at -100.0dBm exactly
------------------------------------------------

A trace containing a level of exactly -100.0dBm has a line reading

	-:.000000e+01

in it.  ':' is the character after '9': the mantissa rounded up to
10.0 and the digit carried off the end of the digit set.  It is
reproducible on every read of such a trace and is not a serial fault,
which matters, because a reader that drops malformed lines silently
loses a point from the sweep exactly where the quietest bins are.
Read as written it is -10.000000e+01, which is the value it meant, and
tinysa.py reads it that way.

What was measured, 22 September 2026
------------------------------------

Six sweeps of 868 to 928MHz, 450 points each, 100kHz resolution
bandwidth, no input attenuation, taken minutes after the board's own
sweeps of the same band from the same bench.

	Floor: -100.4dBm, the median of all 2700 points.
	No bin anywhere in the band is above -90dBm in all six sweeps.
	59 of the 450 bins are above -88dBm in at least one sweep.
	No bin is above it in more than two of the six.
	Each sweep's loudest point is between -69 and -80dBm, and is
	at a different frequency every time: 886.17, 873.75, 871.61,
	886.17, 885.11, 869.60MHz.
	The 59 bins run from 869.47 to 889.38MHz and stop there; the
	upper half of the band, 890 to 928, has none.

So: a quiet band with short bursts in it, scattered in frequency,
never twice in the same place, and confined to the lower half.

The board said the same thing.  Its sweep of the same band found 15
steps of 61 with a peak 28dB above a flat floor and carrier sense
asserted, and the next sweep minutes later found none of them, with
the peak of every step landing at a random point in its dwell rather
than at the start.  Two receivers with nothing in common but the
antenna port they are pointed at -- different front ends, different
calibration, different sweep mechanics, different software by
different people -- both report a quiet floor with intermittent
bursts at scattered frequencies.

That is as much as either can say.  Neither can identify what is
transmitting, and this is not a survey: six sweeps of a minute's
worth of time from one bench.  What it is good for is the thing it was
brought in for -- the board's radio is measuring the room, not
reporting its own imagination.

An activity map, and what it does not say
-----------------------------------------

A sweep visits a frequency for a moment and leaves, so it finds a
transmitter only if the transmitter is on while it is there.  Stepped
zero span does not have that problem: it stays on one frequency and
watches it in the time domain, and a burst anywhere in the window is
seen.  120ms of listening at each of 1 or 2MHz, 300kHz resolution
bandwidth, counting everything more than 12dB over the floor:

	868           silent, peak -91.5
	869           6 bursts
	870 - 878     14 to 49 bursts, peaks -62 to -70, duty 2.5 to 12%
	879, 880      silent, peaks -90.5 and -92.0
	881 - 889     15 to 154 bursts, peaks -67 to -72, duty 1.3 to 27%
	890 - 894     silent, peaks -91 to -92
	894 - 930     silent but for one burst at 912
	820 - 866     silent but for isolated bursts at 820, 822, 824,
	              854, 856

Two blocks eight or nine megahertz wide with hard edges and a two
megahertz gap between them, and nothing else for fifty megahertz
either side.  Transmissions respect allocation edges; noise does not.

At 886MHz the bursts are 400 to 533us long -- six to eight samples of
66.7us -- with a second population at or below one sample, and start
to start gaps with a mode near 200 to 800us and structure out to 5ms.

What has been ruled out.  The tinySA's own spur removal does not
remove them: with "spur on" the floor drops four decibels, as the
feature is meant to, and the bursts and their levels stay.  The input
attenuator could not be used to test them -- "attenuate 10" reads back
as 0 on this instrument in this mode, and neither the peak nor the
noise floor moved when it was set, which is a test that did not run
rather than a test that passed.

What is not established.  Nothing here identifies what is
transmitting.  The band edges are suggestive of an allocation and the
burst lengths of a TDMA system, but two candidate readings were tried
and both were refuted by measurement: a 45MHz FDD pairing would put a
partner block at 825-844 or 915-934 and both are quiet, and a US ISM
reading would put traffic in 902-928 and there is none.

And one disagreement with the board is worth stating.  os/inferno's
sweep found carrier sense asserted at 892, 895, 906, 911, 914, 917,
918, 923, 925 and 928MHz.  The tinySA, dwelling a comparable time at
each of those frequencies, finds nothing there at all.  The two agree
about 870 to 889 and disagree about everything above 890, which makes
the board's hits up there its own -- an image or a spurious response
in the CC1120's front end is the obvious candidate and is not yet
tested.  A receiver that agrees with a second instrument in one half
of a band and not the other has been caught being a receiver, which is
the whole reason for having the second instrument.

listen.py stays on one frequency and prints every burst it hears, for
the two questions a sweep cannot answer -- how long, and how often --
and for watching a frequency while something known is transmitted into
it.  Note its duty: serial round trips mean it is actually listening
for about three percent of the wall clock.

Ultra mode, and an hour spent measuring with the lid on
--------------------------------------------------------

A tinySA ULTRA's low path stops at 800MHz.  Above that it needs
"ultra" mode, and if that is off, everything higher is received badly
or not at all -- not absent, which would be obvious, but attenuated by
twenty-odd decibels, which looks exactly like a weak signal.

That was not checked here until an hour of measurements had been built
on it.  The CC1120 under test transmits at 868MHz.  With ultra off:

	866-870MHz   floor -92    nothing above -84
	600-1000MHz  strongest thing on the air: 767.7MHz at -33

With "ultra on", the same board in the same state:

	866-870MHz   peak -34.9 at 868.00445MHz, 69dB over the floor
	600-1000MHz  868.151 at -34.9; the 767MHz spurs 23dB down

The 767.7MHz emission was real -- it is 24 times the part's 32MHz
crystal, radiated by an enabled amplifier -- and it is below 800MHz,
so it was the one thing being measured properly.  Being the only
honest number in the sweep made it look like the whole story, and a
chain of reasoning was built on its back: that the synthesiser was
unlocked, that the amplifier was oscillating, that the output ignored
the frequency register.  All of it followed from an instrument that
was not measuring the band in question.

tinysa.py now sets "ultra on" before any sweep that reaches past
800MHz.  The rule the episode is worth: an instrument brought in to
check another instrument has settings of its own, and the first thing
to establish about any measurement is whether the thing making it was
able to see what was being measured.


survey.py: what is on a band, rather than what was on it once
=============================================================

A sweep is a snapshot.  With the instrument's default timing a 22MHz
span at 450 points takes 250ms, so each bin is listened to for about
half a millisecond, and a transmitter that sends a 10ms burst once a
minute appears in roughly one sweep in ten thousand.  Sweeping once
and printing the peaks measures the noise and whatever happened to be
talking, and does not tell them apart.  Every "that band is quiet"
verdict in this tree's earlier radio work was reached that way.

survey.py sweeps many times and keeps peak, mean and a count of the
sweeps in which each bin was above the floor.  The three together are
a classifier: a continuous carrier is high in all of them and its peak
and mean are close; a burst transmitter has a high peak, a mean at the
floor and a low count; noise has a peak a few dB up and a mean at the
floor.  Per-bin dwell is bought with "sweeptime" -- measured, one
sweep of a 22MHz span takes 0.99s, 1.61s and 3.64s at settings of
0.25, 1 and 3 -- and the header prints the dwell and the sweep count
so the sensitivity of a negative result is on the page with it.

	survey.py 902000000 928000000 20 1

What it found on this bench, 20 sweeps of 3.8ms a bin:

	868-890   bursty across 869-884, 50kHz to 1.9MHz wide,
	          peaks -75 to -85, never in more than 6 sweeps of 20
	902-928   narrowband bursts 58-116kHz wide, peaks -64 to -85,
	          each seen in one sweep of twelve
	433-435   weak narrow bursts 4 to 18kHz wide, peaks -96 to
	          -102, one sweep in twenty

The 902-928 signals hop.  Four runs put the strongest bin at 922.33,
913.23, 918.56 and 917.63MHz, each 58 to 116kHz wide and each seen
once -- which is frequency hopping and not four different
transmitters.  Zooming in confirms it from the other side: 922.33 read
-64.4dBm in 58kHz bins and the same place reads -90 in 3kHz bins, on a
different bin, because the thing is rarely there.  A CC1120 can
demodulate one hop and cannot follow the sequence without knowing it.

The accumulation is done in the tool and not in the instrument.  The
tinySA has "calc maxh" and "calc aver", which run at the sweep rate
rather than over a serial line and would be the better way if their
trace semantics could be established.  They could not be: "data 0" is
the live sweep and the accumulation appears in "data 1", but "calc
off" does not clear trace 1, an average started after a max hold
decays out of it for tens of seconds instead of converging on the
floor, and a trace read after "calc off" sits 5dB above the live sweep
for no reason that was found.  The first version of this tool used
them and reported a floor of -87.1dBm where the floor is -100.5,
because the average it was reading was still draining a max hold from
the previous run.  Numbers whose meaning is not known are worse than
fewer numbers that are.
